    <h1>Just and Unjust</h1>

    <p>We all have heard these two words, but how many of the times have we thought about them in a deeper way? Most of the people have not yet thought about them. Yet we see that those who don’t even know these in a meaningful sense, appear to be talking about these unnecessarily, without much knowledge.</p>

    <p>There is not a universally accepted. Concept, and this two are obviously one of those. For example, it could be just to treat everyone fairly, but the other person would say that you should treat everyone what they deserve, that is just. One would call cow his mother because he drinks her milk, and call it just. The other would consider it unjust to drink the milk meant for the offspring of cows. One would consider it just to kill a person who was a big criminal. The other would say, you have repeated the exact deed, it is unjust, how could you think is it just?
    Different people apply different standards, those different standards make it hard to decide which one is the right or better one. Yet I would say, universal acceptance is not even required. Because not everyone has equal brains. If we view it from the state’s view, it is unjust what creates disorder. It is just what preserves order. In the eyes of normal human beings, there are lots of standards.</p>

    <p>What is “Just” or “Unjust”? I think what they are is you decide it. Your opinion, your own standard makes it either just or unjust. Sometimes, it is possible you might consider an unlawful ting as just, but to be safe you follow the law. Like it is, in my opinion, unjust to pay taxes as a duty. I consider it as a forced donation. Very unjust. But if tomorrow I have such an income that I have to pay tax on, I would have to pay. This is unjust, but the law is law. Abiding the laws, makes it safer.</p>

    <p>Comment below your “Just” and “Unjust”.</p>

